(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where does the morbidity of basal cell carcinoma come from?","choices":["Transformation into squamous cell carcinoma","Local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites","Nodal metastasis, which occurs in three to seven per cent of all treated cases","Perineural spread to the skull base in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — metastatic disease is rare but serious, and hedgehog inhibitor therapy exists for it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does organ transplantation affect squamous cell carcinoma risk, and when do multiple tumours typically appear?","choices":["It is unchanged by transplantation, since immunosuppressive therapy does not influence keratinocyte carcinoma risk","It is common and often aggressive in transplant recipients, with multiple tumours typically emerging about 5 years after transplant","It is uncommon and usually indolent in transplant recipients, with single tumours typically emerging about 5 years after transplant","It is common and often aggressive in transplant recipients, with multiple tumours typically emerging within 6 weeks of transplant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus also raise risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What are the four possible fates of an individual actinic keratosis?","choices":["It may persist, progress, metastasise to nodes, or recur after treatment","It may only persist or progress","It will always progress if left untreated","It may persist, involute, recur, or progress"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — including involution, which is why individual lesion behaviour is unpredictable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What patient education does the deck give for actinic keratosis?","choices":["Avoid all sun exposure permanently rather than using broad-spectrum protection or protective clothing; stop field therapy at the first sign of erythema or crusting; attend annual review regardless of any change in a lesion","No specific education is needed once the lesions have been treated successfully, since the surrounding field carries no further risk and reactions to therapy are not expected","Daily broad-spectrum sun protection and protective clothing; expect local treatment reactions such as erythema and crusting and adhere to the field therapy duration; seek prompt review of any non-healing or changing lesion","Sun protection in the summer months only and no protective clothing; expect no local reaction to field treatment; return only if an entirely new lesion appears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — warning about the expected reaction protects adherence. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What treatment philosophy does the deck set out for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Total-skin electron-beam treatment as the initial therapy for every patient regardless of stage, because skin-directed topical therapy has no demonstrated benefit","A stage-directed, skin-first approach for most early disease, because early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure or prevent progression and may cause complications and premature death","Immediate aggressive systemic chemotherapy for all stages regardless of extent, because early aggressive treatment reliably cures the disease and prevents progression to systemic involvement","Observation alone at every stage without skin-directed treatment, because no therapy has been shown to alter the course of the disease or to relieve symptoms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — aggression is a harm here, not a virtue. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the overall prognosis of basal cell carcinoma, and where does its morbidity come from?","choices":["Usually rapidly growing and rarely curable even when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from early distant metastasis to lung and liver, with local destruction uncommon","Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from nodal metastasis, which occurs in 3 to 7% of cases, rather than from local destruction","Usually slow-growing but uniformly fatal without systemic therapy; morbidity comes from delayed hedgehog inhibitor treatment rather than from local destruction or recurrence","Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes from local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites, with metastatic disease rare but serious"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the danger is local destruction rather than spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What increases recurrence and functional morbidity in nail unit squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Photographing the lesion before referral","A negative human papillomavirus result","Incomplete excision, immunosuppression, delayed diagnosis and bone invasion","Complete margin-controlled surgery with clear histologic margins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is often curable with complete surgery, which is why incompleteness matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Which two things must a patient on topical therapy for basal cell carcinoma understand?","choices":["That clearance is assumed once the lesion is no longer visible","That sun protection is unnecessary once treatment begins","That local inflammation is expected, and that clearance has to be confirmed afterwards","That inflammation means the drug has failed and that treatment should be stopped early"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the inflammation is not a reason to stop, and clearing visibly is not the same as being clear. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the deck say about amputation in nail unit melanoma?","choices":["It is the automatic first treatment for any nail unit melanoma whatever the depth or extent, since margins cannot be reliably assessed in the nail unit","It is never indicated at all, since digit-sparing wide excision clears every nail unit melanoma regardless of depth, extent or bone involvement","It is reserved for benign nail unit tumours such as glomus tumour or onychomatricoma that recur after local removal","It is NOT automatic — digit-sparing wide excision or Mohs with immunostaining is contemporary care, with amputation reserved for deep, extensive or bone-involving disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the digit is preserved where margins can be reliably assessed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma that fails to respond to antiretroviral therapy at all","An allergic reaction to liposomal doxorubicin","Kaposi sarcoma present when antiretroviral therapy begins can worsen as the immune system recovers","Kaposi sarcoma appearing for the first time several years after antiretroviral therapy begins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the treatment itself can make the disease worse before it improves. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Besides sun exposure and immunosuppression, which risk factors does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Red tattoo ink, laser treatment and cryotherapy","Chronic wounds, scars or prior radiation fields; certain genetic diseases; and mucosal or genital disease","Tanning bed use, fair skin that burns easily, male sex, and outdoor occupation without protection","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three additional categories as listed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype list determines behaviour and treatment?","choices":["The CLINICAL list: superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform subtypes","Either list, since the two are identical","Neither, since size determines treatment","The HISTOLOGIC list: superficial, nodular, micronodular and infiltrative"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and note it differs from the CLINICAL list, which is superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the deck say patients should be told about establishing the cutaneous T-cell lymphoma diagnosis?","choices":["It may require repeated biopsies, and the disease is usually chronic","A single biopsy is definitive in nearly all cases, and the disease is self-limiting","The diagnosis is made clinically and biopsy is unnecessary","Blood testing establishes the diagnosis before any biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — setting that expectation early matters, because the diagnosis is often slow to confirm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype is described as rapidly growing, often amelanotic, and may LACK the classic recognition features?","choices":["Nodular melanoma","Superficial spreading melanoma","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Acral lentiginous melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it may lack ABCDE features, which makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why must oedema severity not be used to judge Kaposi sarcoma burden?","choices":["Oedema only appears once visceral disease is established","Oedema resolves within days of starting treatment","Marked oedema may occur with few or no visible skin lesions","Oedema in Kaposi sarcoma is always caused by the antiretroviral therapy itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck says explicitly not to use oedema severity alone. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How is squamous cell carcinoma in situ managed when there are no high-risk features?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy alone, with no role for topical therapy, curettage or electrodesiccation","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil, or curettage and electrodesiccation in appropriately selected disease","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery in every case, whatever the features and whatever the site","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab in every case, whatever the features and whatever the site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — three options for in situ disease without high-risk features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which lesion features make early squamous cell carcinoma the distinction to exclude in an actinic keratosis?","choices":["Thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent","Rough, flat, flesh-coloured, and more apparent by touch than it is by sight","Pearly, translucent, with telangiectasias","Scaly, symmetric and under two millimetres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck's full list of features that shift the concern to carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which clinical and histologic subtypes of basal cell carcinoma does the deck list, and which determines behaviour?","choices":["Clinical: classic, endemic, iatrogenic, epidemic. Histologic: patch, plaque, tumour. The clinical form determines behaviour","Clinical: superficial, nodular, pigmented, morpheaform. Histologic: superficial, nodular, micronodular, infiltrative. The HISTOLOGIC subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Clinical: superficial, nodular, pigmented, morpheaform. Histologic: superficial, nodular, micronodular, infiltrative. The CLINICAL subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Clinical: superficial spreading, nodular, lentigo maligna, acral. Histologic: in situ, invasive, metastatic. The histologic subtype determines behaviour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — histology drives the treatment decision. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the deck say you should do before assigning a diagnosis to a skin lesion?","choices":["Biopsy every pigmented lesion before any description of type, colour, texture, border or evolution, since morphology is unreliable in premalignant disease","Characterise it systematically — primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and temporal evolution","Assign the most likely diagnosis first, then look for the features that confirm it — colour, texture, border, size and evolution — and discard those that do not fit","Photograph the lesion and defer all description — type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution and evolution — until the dermatology referral has been accepted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Slide 50's diagram maps levels I to V against skin anatomy (this is the system conventionally called the Clark level). Which layers do they correspond to?","choices":["I confined to the epidermis; II into the papillary dermis; III filling the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue","I confined to the epidermis; II into the reticular dermis; III filling the reticular dermis; IV into the papillary dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue","I into the papillary dermis; II into the reticular dermis; III into subcutaneous tissue; IV into muscle; V into bone","I confined to the stratum corneum; II into the epidermis; III into the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the dermis-subcutaneous junction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the level of invasion is an ANATOMIC layer, unlike Breslow thickness which is a measurement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"The deck says to photograph a concerning pigmented band. What caution comes with that?","choices":["Do not photograph it at all, because nail pigment renders inaccurately on a phone camera","Do not photograph it until the polish has regrown","Do not photograph more than one nail at a time","Do not let photographing it delay referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — documentation is for tracking change, not for postponing the specialist. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What caveat does the deck attach to the 6 mm diameter criterion?","choices":["The threshold applies only to lesions on the trunk","The threshold applies only to patients over 50","Smaller lesions can be melanoma","Larger lesions are never melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the threshold is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which findings make referral URGENT in squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["High-risk site, size, recurrence, aggressive histology, immunosuppression, neurologic symptoms, or nodal disease","Cosmetic concern, patient anxiety, a lesion older than one year, a lesion on the trunk, or any pigmented lesion","Only confirmed nodal disease, since high-risk site, size, recurrence and immune status can all be managed routinely","Only failure of two courses of topical therapy, whatever the site, size, histology, immune status or symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — neurologic symptoms suggest perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which findings does the deck list as urgent referral triggers in the nail unit?","choices":["Only a subungual mass confirmed on imaging, since clinical features such as pigment, widening, dystrophy or bleeding cannot themselves indicate urgency","New or changing single-digit longitudinal melanonychia, proximal widening, Hutchinson sign, or nail dystrophy with pigment","Longitudinal pigmented bands present in several nails since childhood, unchanged in width, colour, thickness and spacing over many years, with no nail dystrophy","Any nail change at all in a patient over 65, and any nail that has been abnormal for more than a month, whatever the appearance or the number of digits involved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the list also includes an unexplained subungual mass, persistent ulceration or bleeding, and a chronic wart or infection that fails appropriate therapy. That failed-wart history is the one most easily dismissed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Approximately what proportion of actinic keratosis lesions progresses to squamous cell carcinoma each year?","choices":["About 1 in 1,000 lesions per year","About 1 in 10 lesions per year","About 1 in 100 lesions per year","About 1 in 100,000 lesions per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and cumulative field risk matters more than any single lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In the TNM melanoma staging overview, what do the three letters denote?","choices":["T is primary tumour thickness; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of metastases at a distant site","T is the tumour's anatomic level; N is the number of naevi present; M is the number of mitoses per high-power field","T is the time since onset; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of distant metastases","T is primary tumour thickness; N is the nodal diameter in centimetres; M is the mitotic rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the footnote of the staging table defines all three. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which warning patterns does the deck give for basal cell carcinoma, and where do they commonly appear?","choices":["A sandpaper-textured papule, a pigmented macule larger than 6 mm, or a warty nodule — commonly on the scalp, ears and dorsal hands","A pearly papule, an erythematous patch larger than 6 mm, or a non-healing ulcer — commonly on the palms, soles and nail units","A violaceous macule, a purple plaque, or an oral lesion — commonly on the hard palate and lower limbs","A pearly papule, an erythematous patch larger than 6 mm, or a non-healing ulcer — commonly on the face, trunk or lower legs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three patterns, three common sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What lifetime melanoma risk does the deck give by skin colour?","choices":["About 2% in white individuals; 0.1 to 0.5% in persons of colour","About 20% in white individuals; 1 to 5% in persons of colour","About 2% in white individuals; an equal 2% in persons of colour","About 0.1 to 0.5% in white individuals; about 2% in persons of colour"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lower risk in persons of colour, but not zero. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what are stage 0, stage I and stage II?","choices":["Stage 0 is localised disease only in the skin and very thin; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes","Stage 0 is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is localised disease that is very thin","Stage 0 is localised disease thicker than stage I; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to other organs","Stage 0 is melanoma confined to the epidermal region of the skin; stage I is localised disease, only in skin and very thin; stage II is localised disease thicker than stage I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 0, I and II are all still local. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what distinguishes stage III from stage IV?","choices":["Stage III is localised disease thicker than stage II; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes","Stage III is melanoma confined to the epidermis; stage IV is localised disease in the skin","Stage III is spread to the lymph nodes; stage IV is spread to other organs","Stage III is spread to other organs; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — nodal against distant spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"}]};
